<Base title="Privacy Policy — Pegboard Seven"
      description="Everything Pegboard Seven stores or sends — analytics, ads, purchases and measurement — listed plainly."
      current="legal">

  <section>
    <p class="kicker">Pegboard Seven · Privacy Policy</p>
    <h1 style="max-width:22ch">What the game knows about you.</h1>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="prose">
<p>
  Pegboard Seven is a game. It has no accounts, no sign-in, no messaging and no
  profile. We never ask for your name, your email address or your location, and
  there is nowhere in the app to type any of them.
</p>
<p>
  This page describes <strong>version 1.1</strong>, which added advertising,
  in-app purchases and advertising measurement. What follows is everything the
  app stores or sends, described plainly.
</p>

<h2>Who is responsible</h2>
<p>
  The data controller is <strong>RockPaperScissors</strong>, the trading name
  of Nurçin Ehlimanoğlu, a sole proprietorship registered in Türkiye, reachable at
  <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a>. There is
  no data protection officer: the business is one person, and that person reads
  the address above.
</p>

<h2>What stays on your device</h2>
<p>
  Your progress is saved on the phone itself, using Apple's standard storage
  (<code>UserDefaults</code>). It never leaves the device and we cannot read it.
  It holds exactly this:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>which levels you have unlocked, and the stars on each one</li>
  <li>your coin balance, and which purchases you own</li>
  <li>daily-reward streak, weekly event progress and star chests opened</li>
  <li>which chapter briefings and home-screen benches you have already seen</li>
  <li>your settings: sound, music, haptics, reminders, colour shapes, calm motion and language</li>
  <li>a random identifier created on your device, used as described under <em>Measurement</em></li>
</ul>
<p>
  Deleting the app deletes all of it, and <em>MORE › Reset Progress</em> clears
  progress from inside the game. Purchases you own are held by Apple and come
  back with <em>SHOP › Restore Purchases</em>.
</p>

<h2>What the app sends</h2>

<h3>Analytics — Google Firebase</h3>
<p>
  We record how the game is played so we can tell which levels are unfair and
  where people stop. These are the events, in full:
</p>
<table>
  <tr><th>Event</th><th>What it carries</th></tr>
  <tr><td><code>level_start</code></td><td>level, whether it is a hard level</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>level_complete</code></td><td>level, stars, moves, screws sent to the tray, coins earned</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>level_fail</code>, <code>level_quit</code>, <code>level_skipped</code></td><td>level, reason, moves</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>rescue_offered</code>, <code>rescue_taken</code></td><td>level, reason, coin cost, coin balance</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>booster_used</code></td><td>which booster, coin cost</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>daily_claim</code>, <code>event_claim</code></td><td>day or milestone, coins received</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>ad_shown</code>, <code>interstitial_shown</code>, <code>ad_revenue</code></td><td>ad format, where it was shown, the ad network and what the impression earned</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>iap_click</code>, <code>iap_purchase</code>, <code>iap_failed</code>, <code>iap_restore</code></td><td>which product, its price tier, whether it went through</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>att_answered</code>, <code>notify_asked</code>, <code>rating_asked</code>, <code>language_set</code></td><td>the answer or choice, nothing more</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>exception</code></td><td>a shortened error message, where in the code it happened, level</td></tr>
</table>
<p>
  None of that identifies you. Firebase itself also collects standard technical
  information we do not choose the contents of — an app-instance identifier,
  your device model and operating system version, and a coarse country or region
  worked out from your IP address. The app uses Firebase's variant that has
  <strong>no advertising-identifier support</strong>, so Firebase never reads
  the IDFA. See <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase privacy</a>.
</p>

<h3>Crash reports — Firebase Crashlytics</h3>
<p>
  When the game crashes, a report is sent so the fault can be found. It carries
  the technical state of the crash — the stack trace, your device model and
  iOS version, how much memory was free — and the little we add on purpose:
  which level you were on, whether it was a hard level, and your coin balance.
  There is no name, no address and no account in it, because the game holds none.
</p>

<h3>Purchases — Apple</h3>
<p>
  Coin packs, the starter pack and the ad-free upgrades are sold through the
  App Store. Apple handles the payment; we never see your card, your Apple ID or
  your name. The game receives only a confirmation of which product was bought,
  and records the event above.
</p>

<h3>Advertising</h3>
<p>
  <strong>Pegboard Seven shows advertisements from version 1.1.</strong> They
  appear in two places and nowhere else: a full-screen ad between levels, and an
  ad you choose to watch in exchange for something — emptying a jammed tray,
  extra moves, an extra slot on your next try, skipping a level, doubling a
  level's coins, or free coins in the shop. <strong>There is no banner.</strong>
  Buying either ad-free upgrade stops the full-screen ads; the ads you choose to
  watch stay available.
</p>
<p>
  The ads are filled by <strong>AppLovin MAX</strong>, which runs an auction
  between several ad networks for each slot and shows whichever bids highest.
  The networks that take part are <strong>AppLovin</strong>, the
  <strong>AppLovin Exchange</strong>, <strong>Unity Ads</strong> and
  <strong>Google AdMob</strong>. Each of them receives the request and, with it,
  your device's advertising identifier where you have allowed one, along with
  your approximate location by country, your device model and its operating
  system version. They use it to choose an ad and to count whether it was seen.
</p>

<h3>Measurement — AppsFlyer and Meta</h3>
<p>
  Two further SDKs tell us which advertisement brought someone to the game, so we
  know which campaigns are worth paying for:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>AppsFlyer</strong> receives the install and launches, a handful of
      progress milestones (clearing level 1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 30, 40, 50, 75,
      100, 150, 200, 250 and 300), each purchase with its price and currency, and
      what each ad impression earned. With them goes the random identifier
      created on your device (so the same phone is not counted twice), and your
      advertising identifier where you have allowed it.</li>
  <li><strong>Meta</strong>'s SDK, for advertising we run on Facebook and
      Instagram, receives the install and launches, App Store purchases (read by
      the SDK from Apple's purchase record), the same progress milestones and ad
      impressions. It receives device-level identifiers, not your profile on
      Facebook or Instagram, and the game does not use Facebook Login.</li>
</ul>

<h3>You are asked first</h3>
<p>
  After your first cleared level, iOS asks whether Pegboard Seven may track you
  across other companies' apps and websites. If you decline, the advertising
  identifier is withheld from every SDK above, and ads and measurement work
  without it. In the EEA and the UK you are additionally shown a consent form,
  run by Google's User Messaging Platform, before any personalised advertising
  happens; you can reopen it at any time from <em>MORE › Ad consent</em>.
</p>

<h3>Reminders</h3>
<p>
  If you allow notifications, the game schedules its own reminders on your phone
  (one evening note a day at most). They are created on the device; nothing is
  sent to a server and no push token is collected.
</p>

<h2>Why we are allowed to do this</h2>
<p>
  Under the GDPR the basis for analytics and crash reports is
  <strong>legitimate interest</strong> (Article 6(1)(f)): knowing which levels
  are unfair and which crashes are real is what makes the game fixable, and it
  is done with data that identifies nobody.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>Advertising and measurement are different.</strong> Personalised
  advertising, and the advertising identifier it runs on, happen only where you
  have agreed to them — through the iOS tracking prompt, and in the EEA and the
  UK through the consent form described above. Withhold that and the ads still
  appear, chosen without anything that follows you between apps. Withdrawing it
  later is a setting, not a request to us: <em>MORE › Ad consent</em>, or Apple's
  <em>Settings › Privacy &amp; Security › Tracking</em>.
</p>
<p>
  Purchases are processed to perform the contract you enter when you buy
  (Article 6(1)(b)).
</p>

<h2>How long it is kept</h2>
<p>
  Individual analytics events are held for <strong>2 months</strong>, and the
  anonymous identifier a device is counted under for <strong>14 months</strong>
  after its last event; Google deletes both when the period runs out. Crash
  reports are kept by Crashlytics for around 90 days. Nothing is archived
  elsewhere, and <strong>we never sell or rent any of it</strong>. What the ad
  networks and measurement partners receive is kept under their own retention
  terms, which we do not set and cannot shorten.
</p>

<h2>Where it goes</h2>
<p>
  Firebase is run by Google, and AppLovin, Unity, Google, AppsFlyer and Meta are
  established in the United States; the data leaves Türkiye and the EEA and is
  processed there and wherever else they operate. Each relies on the European
  Commission's standard contractual clauses, and on the EU–US Data Privacy
  Framework where it is certified under it. Their own policies are the authority
  on what they do with what they receive:
  <a href="https://firebase.google.com/support/privacy">Firebase</a>,
  <a href="https://www.applovin.com/privacy/">AppLovin</a>,
  <a href="https://unity.com/legal/game-player-and-app-user-privacy-policy">Unity</a>,
  <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google</a>,
  <a href="https://www.appsflyer.com/legal/services-privacy-policy/">AppsFlyer</a>,
  <a href="https://www.facebook.com/privacy/policy/">Meta</a>.
</p>

<h2>Children</h2>
<p>
  Pegboard Seven is not directed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly
  collect anything from them. There is no chat and no user-generated content.
  If you believe a child's data has reached us through this app, write to
  <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a> and we will
  remove what we can.
</p>

<h2>Your rights</h2>
<p>
  Rights of access, correction, deletion, objection and portability exist under
  the GDPR, the UK GDPR and Türkiye's KVKK (Law 6698). Because we hold nothing
  that identifies you by name, there is usually little for us to look up — but
  write to <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a>
  and we will help as far as the data allows, including with requests to the
  partners named above.
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Stop personalised ads and tracking:</strong> decline the iOS prompt,
      or turn it off later in <em>Settings › Privacy &amp; Security › Tracking</em>;
      in the EEA and UK, <em>MORE › Ad consent</em>.</li>
  <li><strong>Stop analytics and crash reports:</strong> delete the app.</li>
  <li><strong>Delete your progress:</strong> <em>MORE › Reset Progress</em>, or
      delete the app.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  You may also complain directly to a supervisory authority — your national
  authority in the EEA, the ICO in the UK, or the Personal Data Protection
  Authority (KVKK) in Türkiye — without contacting us first.
</p>

<h2>If this changes</h2>
<p>
  If a later version sends anything new, this page is updated before that
  version ships, together with the App Store privacy label. The line below tells
  you which version you are reading about.
</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>
  <a href="mailto:nehlimanoglu@gmail.com">nehlimanoglu@gmail.com</a> — replies
  within three working days.
</p>

